<div class="body"><div class="lookfor"><b>Look for</b>Black Pool's impossible blue · Abyss Pool · Fishing Cone in the lake · elk browsing basin margins</div><p><b>The moment the room bought.</b> Fork RIGHT from the parking path (friend's beta) down to the lakeside walk first. Steam columns off springs draining into the cold lake, low sun across the water, boardwalk empty, ground gurgling.</p><p><b>Beta:</b> One lot at the end of the short spur; boardwalk is a figure-8 — your fork-right move puts you on the lakeside leg first: Fishing Cone sits just offshore, then Black Pool and Abyss Pool (the two impossible blues) anchor the far corner. Full walk is ~0.6 mi. Elk often graze the basin edge at this hour — give them the boardwalk if they’re on it.</p><div class="trail"><b class="h">Trail · West Thumb boardwalk · 0.6 mi figure-8 · flat · 40–60 min</b>Fork RIGHT onto the lakeside leg first (Fishing Cone, Black Pool, Abyss Pool), then take the inner loop on the way back to the lot.</div><a class="maplink" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=44.4166,-110.5732">⌖ Open in Maps</a></div></details></div>

<div class="stop"><input class="ck" type="checkbox" id="sun2"><label class="check" for="sun2">✓</label>
<details><summary><span class="time" data-m="480" data-e="575"><small class="tl">arr</small><b>8:00</b><small>AM</small><em><small class="tl">lv</small><span class="ev">9:35</span></em></span>
<span class="stop-main"><span class="title">Old Faithful + Upper Geyser Basin</span>
<span class="chips"><span class="chip meal">Huckleberry latte</span><span class="chip drive">→ 35 min</span><span class="chip">~1.5 hrs</span></span><span class="flow">1 hr 35 basin + Inn + latte · <em>15 min drive → Fairy Falls lot</em></span></span></summary>
<div class="body"><div class="lookfor"><b>Look for</b>Beehive Geyser if its indicator's going (better show) · Firehole River steaming</div><p>Over Craig Pass — the Continental Divide, twice. Check the eruption board on arrival, walk <b>Geyser Hill</b> while waiting. Then the Inn's 1904 log lobby — 76 feet of lodgepole gothic — and the <b>huckleberry latte</b> at the espresso cart. Buses land ~9:30 as you leave.</p><p><b>Beta:</b> Park in the enormous main lot, then check the prediction board in the visitor center window first — it sets your sequence. Geyser Hill is the 1.3-mi boardwalk loop ACROSS the footbridge behind Old Faithful — if Beehive’s indicator cone is spouting, drop everything and wait (10–15 min warning, taller than Old Faithful, no crowd). The Inn is free to wander: lobby, mezzanine, and the espresso cart is on the lobby’s left side.</p><a class="maplink" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=44.4605,-110.8281">⌖ Open in Maps</a></div></details></div>

<div class="stop"><input class="ck" type="checkbox" id="sun3"><label class="check" for="sun3">✓</label>
<details><summary><span class="time" data-m="590" data-e="630"><small class="tl">arr</small><b>9:50</b><small>AM</small><em><small class="tl">lv</small><span class="ev">10:30</span></em></span>
<span class="stop-main"><span class="title">GRAND PRISMATIC — Fairy Falls Overlook</span>
<span class="chips"><span class="chip drive">→ 15 min</span><span class="chip">0.6 mi walk</span><span class="chip">~45 min</span></span><span class="flow">40 min walk + overlook · <em>15 min drive → Firehole Canyon</em></span></span></summary>
<div class="body"><p>The single most otherworldly color you will ever see outdoors: 370 ft of boiling turquoise ringed in orange — thermophile bacteria mats, each ring a temperature band. Sun's high enough to fire it; the mob is 45 minutes behind you.</p><p><b>Beta:</b> Use the Fairy Falls TRAILHEAD lot, 1 mi south of the Midway lot everyone fights over. Cross the steel bridge, follow the wide gravel road 0.6 mi, then the signed overlook spur climbs left ~100 ft to the platform. Same way back. Total ~1.5 mi — sandals fine, but the boardwalk-brain move is real shoes.</p><div class="trail"><b class="h">Trail · Grand Prismatic Overlook · 1.6 mi RT · ~105 ft · 40–50 min</b>Cross the steel bridge, follow the wide old roadbed 0.6 mi, then the signed overlook spur climbs LEFT ~0.2 mi to the platform. Running ahead of schedule? The main trail continues 0.8 mi to Fairy Falls itself — a 197-ft ribbon — adding ~1.6 mi RT / 45 min.</div><a class="maplink" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=44.5155,-110.8325">⌖ Fairy Falls trailhead</a></div></details></div>

<div class="stop"><input class="ck" type="checkbox" id="sun4"><label class="check" for="sun4">✓</label>
<details><summary><span class="time" data-m="645" data-e="680"><small class="tl">arr</small><b>10:45</b><small>AM</small><em><small class="tl">lv</small><span class="ev">11:20</span></em></span>
<span class="stop-main"><span class="title">Firehole Canyon — Swim #2 (if open)</span>
<span class="chips"><span class="chip swim">Swim #2 · verify first</span><span class="chip drive">→ 15 min</span><span class="chip">45 min</span></span><span class="flow">35 min swim window · <em>40 min drive → Norris</em></span></span></summary>
<div class="body"><p>Thermally-warmed river (high 70s–80s°) in a lava canyon — the one warm swim in the park. <b>It was closed ALL of 2025</b> for high water, and 2026 status was unconfirmed at press time, so verify before counting on it:</p><p><b>1.</b> Park app / nps.gov Alerts while you still have signal Saturday · <b>2.</b> Ask the ranger at the East gate or the Grant desk at check-in — they'll know instantly · <b>3.</b> The lot itself: towels on rocks = yes, barricade = scenic drive.</p><p>Drive the 2-mi Firehole Canyon spur regardless — canyon walls + Firehole Falls make it worth it even dry. If swimming: real current, no lifeguard, stay in the main pool with everyone else, sandals on (volcanic rock bottom).</p><a class="maplink" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=Firehole+Canyon+Drive+swimming+area">⌖ Open in Maps</a></div></details></div>

<div class="stop"><input class="ck" type="checkbox" id="sun5"><label class="check" for="sun5">✓</label>
<details><summary><span class="time" data-m="720" data-e="830"><small class="tl">arr</small><b>12:00</b><small>PM</small><em><small class="tl">lv</small><span class="ev">1:50</span></em></span>
<span class="stop-main"><span class="title">NORRIS GEYSER BASIN — both loops</span>
<span class="chips"><span class="chip drive">→ 40 min</span><span class="chip">~2 hrs</span></span><span class="flow">1 hr 50 both loops · <em>25 min drive → Canyon fuel stop</em></span></span></summary>
<div class="body"><div class="lookfor"><b>Look for</b>Steamboat's runoff channel (world's tallest geyser) · the hiss of Porcelain Basin</div><p>The finale — hottest, oldest, most volatile ground in the park. <b>Porcelain Basin first</b>: the alien one, a treeless bowl of vents and milky-blue runoff. Then Back Basin past Steamboat. Artists Paintpots (1-mi loop) optional en route if ahead of schedule.</p><p><b>Beta:</b> Both loops leave from the little museum at the lot: Porcelain Basin (0.8 mi) drops north into the alien bowl — do it first — then Back Basin (1.5 mi) loops south past Steamboat (check its runoff channel: wet and steaming means it’s been active) and Echinus. Noon lot takes patience; a spot always opens. Bookstore at the museum for the one souvenir that’s not junk.</p><div class="trail"><b class="h">Trail · Norris loops · 2.3 mi total · ~1.5–2 hr</b>Porcelain Basin loop first: 0.8 mi dropping north from the museum into the bowl, 30–40 min. Then the Back Basin loop: 1.5 mi — walk it counterclockwise so Steamboat and Emerald Spring come early, 45–60 min.</div><a class="maplink" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=44.7264,-110.7031">⌖ Open in Maps</a></div></details></div>

