<div class="body"><div class="lookfor"><b>Look for</b>Peak August wildflowers · Beartooth Butte’s striped face over the water · marmots on the granite · trout rising in Beauty Lake</div><p>Gentle rolling trail through meadows at 9,000 ft — gorgeous within ten minutes, Beauty Lake’s granite bowl a mile-plus in. <b>She sets the pace and the turnaround.</b></p><p><b>THE SWIM:</b> Beauty Lake is the swimmable one up here — shallower than its neighbors, so the top few feet genuinely warm by August afternoons (brisk-but-doable, not plunge-only). Your 2:30–4:15 window is the warmest water of the day. Look for the sandy/granite shelf entries on the near shore — walk-in, no rock scramble. Suits under clothes so there’s no trailhead changing act; mom can wade the shelf while you swim; stay near shore (it’s still 9,400 ft — cold makes swimmers overestimate themselves). Out, towel, dry layer immediately: the breeze up there turns wet-and-happy into cold-and-done in five minutes.</p><p>Then the picnic on the granite, feet drying in the sun, trout rising. That’s the trip’s highlight reel, right there.</p><p><b>Beta:</b> Trailhead is at the Beartooth Lake boat launch/campground turnoff — park by the ramp. The Beauty Lake trail starts along the lake’s east shore, crosses the inlet creek early (rock-hop or ankle splash by August — water shoes shine here), then climbs gently through the meadows. At the junction, stay RIGHT for Beauty Lake. The sandy entries are where the trail first meets the lake.</p><div class="trail"><b class="h">Trail · Beauty Lake Trail · ~2.8 mi RT · ~300 ft · ~1.5 hr walking at mom pace</b>From the boat launch, follow Beartooth Lake’s east shore north ~½ mi · rock-hop the inlet creek (water shoes shine) · cross the wildflower meadows · at the signed junction around the 1-mi mark stay RIGHT · Beauty Lake arrives ~1.4 mi in, and the first shoreline the trail touches has the sandy swim entries. Same trail back — and remember, turning around early anywhere on this is a complete hike.</div><p><i>Legs vote no at the trailhead? Clay Butte fire lookout is 5 min further — drive-up 360° panorama, hundred-yard walk. The day delivers at zero hiking.</i></p><a class="maplink" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=Beartooth+Lake+Trailhead">⌖ Open in Maps</a></div></details></div>

